[469.312283, "o", "Done. `/app/model.xml` now simulates the same 2-second scene in **~46% of the reference time** (2.18× speedup), well under the 60% target.\r\n\r\n## What I changed\r\n\r\nOnly solver/linear-algebra options — no physical properties, geometry, joints, damping, masses, timestep, or plugins were altered:\r\n\r\n```xml\r\n<option jacobian=\"dense\" solver=\"PGS\" tolerance=\"1e-12\"/>\r\n```\r\n\r\nWhy it works:\r\n\r\n- **`jacobian=\"dense\"`** — profiling showed the serial cable's mass matrix is ~98% dense, so the default auto/sparse Cholesky was paying fill-in overhead. Dense factorization is cheaper here.\r\n- **`solver=\"PGS\"` with `tolerance=\"1e-12\"`** — the constraint solve (one `connect` equality) dominated runtime under Newton because of its warmstart/factor/solve overhead. PGS converges to the same solution with far less work; the tight tolerance keeps the result essentially exact.\r\n\r\n## Validation\r\n\r\n- `eval.py` passes repeatedly: state diff prints `0.0000`, time pctg **0.46**.\r\n- Stress-tested **300 random seeds**: maximum absolu"]
[469.312314, "o", "te state difference **4.25e-8** vs `model_ref.xml` (tolerance is 1e-5, ~235× margin), no NaN/Inf, zero failures.\r\n- `/app/model_ref.xml` is untouched.\r\n"]
